# mypki .gitignore
#
# Two rules govern everything below. (1) Runtime state never belongs in git: the CA's key material, the generated session
# secret, the issued ledger and every log line are produced by running the thing, and a commit that captures them is a leak
# with a diff attached. (2) The key-material block is deliberately broad (`*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.crt`, `*.csr`, `*.p12`) rather
# than narrowed to `<data>/ca/`, because the mistake it protects against is an operator running `mypki init-ca` in the wrong
# directory — which puts the CA key exactly here, and no amount of path-specific ignoring would notice.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
build/
dist/
sdist/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg
.eggs/
MANIFEST

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- virtualenvs
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- tool caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
*.cover
.tox/
.nox/
.pre-commit-config.yaml.bak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- editors / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- gate stamp
# `.gate-ready` is written by `make ready` and read by `make gate`: one line naming the tree that the next gate is
# allowed to measure (HEAD^{tree}, a digest over the contents of every listed path, and the tracked path count). It must
# stay ignored, and not only for tidiness — re-measured on 2026-11-08 with every other file held constant: putting
# `.gate-ready` back in the list `git ls-files --cached --exclude-standard --others` answers grows the path count 78 -> 79
# and changes the digest half (reproduce it by adding a `!/.gate-ready` line here, running `tools/tree_manifest.py
# --fingerprint`, then deleting the line — the count and digest both return). No digest hex is quoted, because a hex quoted in prose is false the moment any other file in the
# digest changes — the same staleness this repository's generated README block exists to avoid. A gate that stamps its own
# tree would invalidate its own stamp, and README's regenerated count would grow by one on every run: a guard that
# rejects itself.
.gate-ready

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- generated reports
# `reports/harness-ledger.json` is written by tools/e2e_harness.py at the end of its own summary -- totals and per-section
# pass/fail/warn counts for this machine's run, no longer re-derived from a log file by a second tool. Committing it would
# freeze one box's numbers into the repository as if they were a property of the release. The documents point at this path
# and say how to read it instead of copying numbers out of it.
reports/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- runtime state
logs/
*.log
*.log.[0-9]*
/data/
instance/
issued.db
issued.db-*
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- key material
# Never commit any of these. `mypki-root-ca.crt` is a PUBLIC certificate and is still ignored, because the download exists
# to be fetched from the running CA rather than frozen in a repository where it silently expires.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.csr
*.p12

# scratch output from ad-hoc background runs (never a product artifact)
nohup.out
